=begin pod

=head1 Write and export API documentation

Document public declarations with RakuDoc declarator comments directly above
their definitions. Describe the result, whether the operation performs I/O,
ownership and cleanup, and errors the caller can handle. Keep longer examples
in the executable examples directory and link to them rather than maintaining
an unchecked second copy.

Use native code markup and links inside declarator comments:

=begin code :lang<raku>
#| Inspect a C<Snapshot>. See L<LibTmux::Model::Snapshot>.
=end code

Native links must resolve to one exported declaration or an
explicit signature value type. Spell an ambiguous target fully. Unknown
or ambiguous targets fail native extraction as well as the shared adapter.
Resolution checks exact identities, exported aliases, the owner's scope, then
a unique declaration name. The exporter validates targets without choosing
website routes.
Use link labels without square brackets; the current shared Markdown bridge
cannot represent them, so native extraction rejects them explicitly.

Use the RakuDoc V1 syntax supported by the pinned compiler. Native declarator
comments, C<C> markup and C<L> links remain the authoring source; the shared site
is a renderer of that source.

=head2 Native extraction

Run this command from the repository root after installing the declared
dependencies and selecting the compiler pinned in C<.tool-versions>:

=begin code :lang<console>
$ mise exec -- raku -I. tools/export-docs.raku
=end code

The author tool writes C<docs/_build/api.json>. It parses trusted source with
Rakudo's RakuAST and reads native RakuDoc nodes; parsing can execute compile-time
code. The exporter reads the public import, independently public modules,
signature value types, and typed exceptions. It copies inherited native
declarations onto their public owners.
Internal transport owners do not become public API merely because they occur
in C<META6.json>.

The artifact records exact native signatures, resolved type references,
source lines, RakuDoc links, and the generated field catalogue. Library and
exporter identities are separate, with content digests and dirty-tree flags.
It embeds the captured module sources and refuses an input or HEAD change
during extraction.

Check the native extraction fixture separately:

=begin code :lang<console>
$ mise exec -- prove6 -I. xt/docs/export.rakutest
=end code

Check documentation coverage after a full export:

=begin code :lang<console>
$ jq --exit-status '(.missingDocumentation | type == "object" and length == 0)' docs/_build/api.json
=end code

Missing prose is reported by declaration with its source file and line.
An empty report establishes coverage; it does not establish that the prose
correctly describes behavior. Behavioral review remains required.

=head2 Capability directory

The shared site folds the C<What is on here> section of the C<LibTmux::Server>
class documentation into a table. Write each row as a paragraph that starts
with a bold title, then bullets that each hold a short label, a colon and
method names as code spans. List every public method and property in a bullet,
with an C<-async> method beside its synchronous twin. The export fixture test
extracts the Server module and fails, naming the member, when one is missing.

=head2 Executable examples

Complete programs in C<examples/> are the canonical source. The README marks a
complete fenced block with C<< <!-- runs: examples/query-panes.raku --> >>.
The native guide uses an abbreviated RakuDoc comment,
C<=comment runs: examples/query-panes.raku>, immediately before its code block.
The runner checks every marked program byte-for-byte and rejects unmarked Raku
blocks in those guides. Check source alignment without starting tmux:

=begin code :lang<console>
$ mise exec -- raku tools/check-examples.raku --check-source
=end code

The native exporter captures all shipped example files with their input digests
and source bytes. A rendered example therefore links to the captured version.
The example runner executes every program, checks its result, and rejects
leftover owned socket directories. Set C<LIBTMUX_TEST_TMUX> to the absolute
endpoint executable and run:

=begin code :lang<console>
$ mise exec -- raku tools/check-examples.raku
=end code

Run the programs to validate their output; compilation alone checks syntax and
compile-time behavior. RakuDoc code blocks have no automatic doctest gate.
Link displayed programs to the tested files or check that their source matches.

=head2 Shared rendering

The shared documentation repository consumes the versioned
C<libtmux.raku-api/v1> artifact. Its adapter validates input digests, parent
relationships, native signature spans, and cross-links before rendering.
The library exporter revision and shared adapter revision remain separate.

A dirty source tree requires explicit local-preview mode. Source links in
that preview open the exact captured files, and pages identify the preview
as uncommitted. Follow the repository boundaries in C<AGENTS.md> for publication.

=end pod
